appropriate word, we can use the programs we had built for parsing to do
generation instead.  Implement Alyssa's idea, and show the first half-dozen or
so sentences generated.@footnote{Although Alyssa's idea works just fine (and is
surprisingly simple), the sentences that it generates are a bit boring---they
don't sample the possible sentences of this language in a very interesting way.
In fact, the grammar is highly recursive in many places, and Alyssa's technique
``falls into'' one of these recursions and gets stuck.  See @ref{Exercise 4.50}
for a way to deal with this.}
@end quotation

@node	4.3.3, 4.4, 4.2, 4.3
@subsection Implementing the @code{Amb} Evaluator

The evaluation of an ordinary Scheme expression may return a value, may never
terminate, or may signal an error.  In nondeterministic Scheme the evaluation
of an expression may in addition result in the discovery of a dead end, in
which case evaluation must backtrack to a previous choice point.  The
interpretation of nondeterministic Scheme is complicated by this extra case.

We will construct the @code{amb} evaluator for nondeterministic Scheme by
modifying the analyzing evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}.@footnote{We chose to
implement the lazy evaluator in @ref{4.2} as a modification of the
ordinary metacircular evaluator of @ref{4.1.1}.  In contrast, we will
base the @code{amb} evaluator on the analyzing evaluator of
@ref{4.1.7}, because the execution procedures in that evaluator provide a
convenient framework for implementing backtracking.}  As in the analyzing
evaluator, evaluation of an expression is accomplished by calling an execution
procedure produced by analysis of that expression.  The difference between the
interpretation of ordinary Scheme and the interpretation of nondeterministic
Scheme will be entirely in the execution procedures.

@subsubheading Execution procedures and continuations

Recall that the execution procedures for the ordinary evaluator take one
argument: the environment of execution.  In contrast, the execution procedures
in the @code{amb} evaluator of this edition take two: the environment, and one
procedure called a @newterm{continuation procedure}, the @newterm{success
continuation}.  The evaluation of an expression will finish by calling the
success continuation with a value; if the evaluation results in the discovery
of a dead end, it performs the @code{Fail} effect, which takes the place of the
book's @newterm{failure continuation}: instead of a procedure to call, the dead
end is a signal that unwinds the host stack to the innermost handler installed
for the branch.  Constructing the success continuations, and installing
the handlers that intercept @code{Fail}, is the mechanism by which the
nondeterministic evaluator implements backtracking.

It is the job of the success continuation to receive a value and proceed with
the computation.  Because the call is nested, the whole future use of the value
-- and of every value chosen after it -- stays inside the dynamic extent of the
choice handlers that were installed for the branch, so a failure raised
anywhere in that future unwinds into exactly the handler that can advance the
search.

A failure is triggered during evaluation when a user program explicitly rejects
the current line of attack (for example, a call to @code{require} may result in
execution of @code{(amb)}, an expression that always fails---see
@ref{4.3.1}), or when a choice point runs out of alternatives.  The
@code{Fail} effect unwinds to the innermost enclosing branch handler: an
assignment trail interceptor on the way, then the most recent choice point,
which tries its next alternative, or the driver when no choice is pending.

In addition, if a side-effect operation (such as assignment to a variable)
occurs on a branch of the process resulting from a choice, it may be necessary,
when the process finds a dead end, to undo the side effect before making a new
choice.  This is accomplished by having the side-effect operation install an
interceptor that undoes the assignment and then propagates the failure.

In summary, @code{Fail} handlers are installed by

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{amb} expressions---to provide a mechanism to make alternative choices if
the current choice made by the @code{amb} expression leads to a dead end;

@item
assignments---to intercept failures and undo assignments during backtracking,
unless the assignment was made by @code{permanent-set!} (exercise
@ref{Exercise 4.51});

@item
@code{if-fail} expressions (exercise @ref{Exercise 4.52})---to convert the
first failure of an expression into the value of a fallback expression.

@end itemize

@noindent
Failures are initiated only when a dead end is encountered.  This occurs

@itemize @bullet

@item
if the user program executes @code{(amb)};

@item
if the driver's @code{try_again} is answered by a search that has run past its
last suspension.

@end itemize

@subsubheading Structure of the evaluator

The syntax- and data-representation procedures of the @code{amb} evaluator are
those of the substrate of @ref{4.1.7}, except for the fact that the new
special forms are recognized at application heads by their reserved names --
@code{amb}, @code{permanent-set!}, and @code{if-fail} -- which is the same
recognition by head symbol the book's @code{tagged-list?} predicates perform;
the shared grammar gains no node kinds.@footnote{We assume
that the evaluator supports @code{let} (see @ref{Exercise 4.22}), which we have
used in our nondeterministic programs; this edition evaluates @code{let} as a
simultaneous binding inside the dispatch.}  The clauses of the evaluator are
the functor @code{Core}, parameterized by the continuation-passing evaluation
step its clauses recurse through, exactly as in @ref{4.1}:

@example ocaml
type exec = Value.env -> (Value.t -> unit) -> unit

type eval_k = Ast.expr -> Value.env -> (Value.t -> unit) -> unit

type _ Effect.t += Fail : unit Effect.t
type _ Effect.t += Amb : exec list -> unit Effect.t
type _ Effect.t += Answer : Value.t -> unit Effect.t
@end example

@noindent
The three effects are the whole search protocol.  @code{Fail} is a dead end.
@code{Amb} carries the analyzed alternatives of one choice point to the
@code{choice} handler.  @code{Answer} delivers a top-level success to the
driver's sink.

@subsubheading Choice points

The @code{amb} special form analyzes its alternatives and performs @code{Amb}
carrying them; the @code{choice} handler runs the alternatives in order under
the success continuation in hand, intercepting a @code{Fail} from a tried
alternative -- including the whole future use of its value, which stays nested
in the call -- and moving to the next one.  An exhausted list performs
@code{Fail} to the enclosing branch:

@example ocaml
let choice (alternatives : exec list) : exec =
 fun env succeed ->
  let rec try_next = function
    | [] -> Effect.perform Fail
    | first :: rest ->
      Effect.Deep.match_with (fun env -> first env succeed) env
        @{ retc = (fun () -> try_next rest)
        ; exnc = raise
        ; effc =
            (fun (type a) (eff : a Effect.t) ->
              match eff with
              | Fail ->
                Some
                  (fun (_dead : (a, _) continuation) ->
                    incr backtracks;
                    try_next rest)
              | _ -> None)
        @}
  in
  try_next alternatives
;;
@end example

@noindent
The counter the handler increments is exercise @ref{Exercise 4.44a}'s
backtrack counter.

@subsubheading Assignments

Assignments are where the undo trail lives.  The execution procedure for
assignments first obtains the new value; then it saves the old value, makes the
assignment, and runs the success that follows inside an interceptor -- a
@code{Fail} performed later in the branch restores the old value before
propagating.  This is the book's starred success and failure continuations,
expressed as one handler:@footnote{We
didn't worry about undoing definitions, since we can assume that internal
definitions are scanned out (@ref{4.1.6}).}

@example ocaml
(* [eval_assignment name exp env succeed] *)
Eval.eval_k exp env (fun value ->
    let old = lookup_binding env name in
    set_binding env name value;
    try succeed (Value.symbol "ok") with
    | effect Fail, _dead ->
      set_binding env name old;
      Effect.perform Fail)
@end example

@noindent
Exercise @ref{Exercise 4.51}'s @code{permanent-set!} is the same clause
without the interceptor: the assignment survives every backtracking step.

@subsubheading The driver and the try-again protocol

The driver keeps the search state across answers.  A top-level success
performs @code{Answer} at the sink; the driver's handler stashes the
continuation of that perform -- a one-shot host continuation, resumed exactly
once by @code{try_again}, which is why one capture yields exactly one further
answer -- and reports the value.  Resuming the stashed search lets the sink
return, and the answer's production path unwinds into the choice handlers, so
the search moves on exactly as the book's driver loop does when it re-invokes
the failure branch to find additional values.  A @code{Fail} that reaches the
driver means the choices are exhausted:

@example ocaml
let rec driver_handler
    : (unit, (Value.t, Eval_error.t) result) Effect.Deep.handler =
  @{ retc = (fun () -> pending := None; Error no_more_values)
  ; exnc = (fun e ->
      match e with
      | Raised error -> pending := None; Error error
      | other -> raise other)
  ; effc =
      (fun (type a) (eff : a Effect.t) ->
        match eff with
        | Answer v ->
          Some (fun (k : (a, (Value.t, Eval_error.t) result) continuation) ->
              pending := Some k;
              Ok v)
        | Fail ->
          Some (fun _dead -> pending := None; Error no_more_values)
        | _ -> None) @}
and try_again () =
  match !pending with
  | None -> Error (Eval_error.User_error "there is no current problem")
  | Some k ->
    pending := None;
    (* resuming re-enters the driver's handler: its answer to the next
       Answer perform is exactly this call's result *)
    Effect.Deep.continue k ()
@end example

@noindent
@code{Run} evaluates one expression as one problem under this handler: the
value of its first non-failing execution, or the typed exhaustion error.

